<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?>
<assembly xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v1" manifestVersion="1.0">

  <assemblyIdentity
      type="win32"
      name="ResProbe.DisplayDiagnostics"
      version="1.0.0.0"
      processorArchitecture="amd64"/>

  <description>ResProbe display resolution and remote desktop diagnostics</description>

  <!--
    WARNING: never put two consecutive hyphens inside an XML comment.
    XML forbids that sequence in comments, and an illegal comment makes the whole
    manifest invalid, which shows up only at process start as:
        "The application has failed to start because its side by side
         configuration is incorrect."
    Use "=" or "~" if you want a separator line. This trap has been hit twice,
    which is why res/check-manifest.ps1 runs as part of the build.

    requireAdministrator
    ====================
    This tool is an administrator tool by design:
      - deviceinstaller64.exe install / enableidd  (virtual display driver)
      - SetDisplayConfig / ChangeDisplaySettingsEx (display topology)
      - schtasks /create /rl highest               (autostart)

    The .cmd wrappers already refuse to run without elevation (fltmc probe), so
    requireAdministrator does not change what the tool can do. It only replaces a
    confusing API failure with a clear UAC prompt.

    Effect on the .cmd wrappers:
    Elevating an already elevated child does NOT create a new process, so when a
    wrapper script (itself elevated) calls resprobe.exe, stdout redirection and
    the exit code keep working. That is the contract the scripts rely on, see
    DEVELOPMENT.md section 6.5. Launching resprobe.exe from a NON elevated console
    does spawn a separate elevated process: output goes to a new console and the
    exit code is lost. That is expected and documented.

    To make the probe subcommands runnable without elevation, change the level
    below to "asInvoker" and rebuild. Tray menu items that need admin will then
    fail instead of prompting.
  -->
  <trustInfo xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v3">
    <security>
      <requestedPrivileges>
        <requestedExecutionLevel level="requireAdministrator" uiAccess="false"/>
      </requestedPrivileges>
    </security>
  </trustInfo>

  <compatibility xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:compatibility.v1">
    <application>
      <!--
        Only Windows 10 and 11 are declared, matching what the code actually
        targets (_WIN32_WINNT = 0x0A00 in src/common.h) and what README states.

        Earlier revisions also listed Windows 7, 8 and 8.1. That was misleading:
        the tool depends on SetDisplayConfig and on the IddCx indirect display
        framework, and the bundled usbmmidd v2 driver is Windows 10 only.
        Listing an OS here does NOT add support for it, it only changes how the
        loader reports the version. Declaring systems the tool cannot serve
        invites a confusing "it installed but does nothing" report, so those
        entries are dropped.
      -->
      <!-- Windows 10 and 11 -->
      <supportedOS Id="{8e0f7a12-bfb3-4fe8-b9a5-48fd50a15a9a}"/>
    </application>
  </compatibility>

  <application xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v3">
    <windowsSettings>
      <dpiAware xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2005/WindowsSettings">true</dpiAware>
      <dpiAwareness xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2016/WindowsSettings">system</dpiAwareness>
    </windowsSettings>
  </application>

</assembly>
